WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:12.390
In this video, you expand the disk of a running virtual machine without stopping it, then grow the partition and filesystem inside the guest so the new space is usable.

00:00:12.390 --> 00:00:20.450
The virtual machine demo-resize is running. Its root disk, demo-resize-disk-0, is ten gigabytes.

00:00:20.450 --> 00:00:24.870
On the Volumes page, open the disk's action menu,

00:00:24.870 --> 00:00:29.770
and select Edit Config. You don't stop the virtual machine.

00:00:29.770 --> 00:00:35.070
Change the size to twelve gigabytes. A disk can grow but never shrink.

00:00:35.070 --> 00:00:41.870
Select Save. Longhorn expands the volume while the virtual machine keeps running.

00:00:41.870 --> 00:00:45.280
The volume now shows twelve gigabytes.

00:00:45.280 --> 00:00:51.790
The guest still sees the old partition size. Open the virtual machine's console,

00:00:51.790 --> 00:00:55.470
in WebVNC.

00:00:55.470 --> 00:00:57.650
Log in as sles,

00:00:57.650 --> 00:01:01.580
with the password set by cloud-init.

00:01:01.580 --> 00:01:12.200
lsblk shows the disk is now twelve gigabytes, but the root partition, vda3, still has the old size.

00:01:12.200 --> 00:01:26.550
Grow partition three into the free space, then grow the X F S root filesystem. Both work while it is mounted.

00:01:26.550 --> 00:01:36.530
The root filesystem now has the extra space, and the virtual machine never stopped.

00:01:36.530 --> 00:01:43.509
You expanded a disk online. Next, you take and restore a virtual machine snapshot.
